        ['id'=>9315,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>3114,'title'=>'serious Becomes Fastest-Growing Economy in Africa at fifteen% GDP Growth','exc'=>'The speed of the takeover left little time for a coordinated international response, with regional bodies convening emergency sessions as the full picture of events became clearer.','body'=>'<p>The situation developed with a speed that left observers struggling to keep pace, as each new development built on the last in ways that underscored the interconnected nature of the forces at work.</p><p>Those with deep expertise in the area described it as a confluence of trends that had each been building independently for years before arriving simultaneously at a point of acute pressure.</p><p>The institutional response was measured, with officials taking care to frame their position in a way that acknowledged the complexity without appearing to minimise the significance of what was unfolding.</p><p>What comes next remains genuinely uncertain, though the structural conditions that produced the current moment show no sign of resolving themselves quickly or easily.</p>','cat'=>'World News','author'=>'Marcus Webb','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1588681664899-f142ff2dc9b1?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Sahel','Security','Conflict'],'date'=>'2022-10-26 18:34:00'],
