        ['id'=>9629,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>4211,'title'=>'Energy Infrastructure Targeted: 180 Power Stations Knocked Offline','exc'=>'The aid package, the largest of its kind since the conflict began, cleared a legislative hurdle after weeks of debate over the fiscal and diplomatic implications of continued support.','body'=>'<p>The announcement was received with a range of reactions that reflected genuinely different assessments of the evidence, the stakes, and the appropriate weight to give competing considerations.</p><p>Supporters argued that the decision represented a rational response to conditions that could not be indefinitely ignored, while critics maintained that the approach risked unintended consequences that had not been adequately modelled.</p><p>Both positions had merit, and the honest answer was that the situation involved real trade-offs between values and interests that did not admit of a perfect resolution — only better or worse choices among imperfect options.</p><p>The debate continued in the days that followed, with new information and perspectives adding detail to a picture that remained incomplete and in certain key respects contested.</p>','cat'=>'World News','author'=>'Katerina Polak','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1551009175-15bfef3ae1de?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Sanctions','Europe','Conflict'],'date'=>'2024-06-25 15:44:00'],
