        ['id'=>10071,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>907,'title'=>'Critical Minerals Agreement Signed by sweeping Nations Excluding China','exc'=>'Economists divided on whether the tariff escalation would achieve its stated strategic objectives, with many arguing the costs to domestic consumers would outweigh any competitive advantage gained.','body'=>'<p>The announcement was received with a range of reactions that reflected genuinely different assessments of the evidence, the stakes, and the appropriate weight to give competing considerations.</p><p>Supporters argued that the decision represented a rational response to conditions that could not be indefinitely ignored, while critics maintained that the approach risked unintended consequences that had not been adequately modelled.</p><p>Both positions had merit, and the honest answer was that the situation involved real trade-offs between values and interests that did not admit of a perfect resolution — only better or worse choices among imperfect options.</p><p>The debate continued in the days that followed, with new information and perspectives adding detail to a picture that remained incomplete and in certain key respects contested.</p>','cat'=>'Business','author'=>'Oliver Grant','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1486406146926-c627a92ad1ab?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Trade','Tariffs','WTO'],'date'=>'2024-03-05 13:09:00'],
