        ['id'=>10553,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>4433,'title'=>'SpaceX Starship Completes First Successful Orbital Flight and Landing','exc'=>'The commercial model for the mission was without precedent and raised questions that the industry had not yet fully resolved about liability, ownership, and the applicability of existing international space law.','body'=>'<p>The announcement was received with a range of reactions that reflected genuinely different assessments of the evidence, the stakes, and the appropriate weight to give competing considerations.</p><p>Supporters argued that the decision represented a rational response to conditions that could not be indefinitely ignored, while critics maintained that the approach risked unintended consequences that had not been adequately modelled.</p><p>Both positions had merit, and the honest answer was that the situation involved real trade-offs between values and interests that did not admit of a perfect resolution — only better or worse choices among imperfect options.</p><p>The debate continued in the days that followed, with new information and perspectives adding detail to a picture that remained incomplete and in certain key respects contested.</p>','cat'=>'Technology','author'=>'Diego Reyes','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1504384308090-c894fdcc538d?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['NASA','Moon','Artemis'],'date'=>'2023-03-25 13:07:00'],
