        ['id'=>10778,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>1804,'title'=>'SpaceX Starship Completes First Successful Orbital Flight and Landing','exc'=>'Mission controllers described the moment as one that validated years of iteration, failure, and redesign, saying it opened the door to a cadence of flights that would have seemed implausible just years before.','body'=>'<p>The announcement was received with a range of reactions that reflected genuinely different assessments of the evidence, the stakes, and the appropriate weight to give competing considerations.</p><p>Supporters argued that the decision represented a rational response to conditions that could not be indefinitely ignored, while critics maintained that the approach risked unintended consequences that had not been adequately modelled.</p><p>Both positions had merit, and the honest answer was that the situation involved real trade-offs between values and interests that did not admit of a perfect resolution — only better or worse choices among imperfect options.</p><p>The debate continued in the days that followed, with new information and perspectives adding detail to a picture that remained incomplete and in certain key respects contested.</p>','cat'=>'Technology','author'=>'Priya Desai','img'=>'','tags'=>['Satellite','Starlink','Internet'],'date'=>'2022-08-12 05:51:00'],
