        ['id'=>11609,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>true,'views'=>4851,'title'=>'Tipping Point Triggered in 300: Cascading Effects Now Inevitable','exc'=>'The researchers described the finding as deeply troubling, noting that it implied a remaining budget for emissions far smaller than the figures that had been used to frame current policy discussions.','body'=>'<p>The situation developed with a speed that left observers struggling to keep pace, as each new development built on the last in ways that underscored the interconnected nature of the forces at work.</p><p>Those with deep expertise in the area described it as a confluence of trends that had each been building independently for years before arriving simultaneously at a point of acute pressure.</p><p>The institutional response was measured, with officials taking care to frame their position in a way that acknowledged the complexity without appearing to minimise the significance of what was unfolding.</p><p>What comes next remains genuinely uncertain, though the structural conditions that produced the current moment show no sign of resolving themselves quickly or easily.</p>','cat'=>'Science','author'=>'Prof James Okonkwo','img'=>'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1446776899648-aa78eefe8ed0?w=800&q=80','tags'=>['Ocean','Temperature','Measurement'],'date'=>'2024-08-04 05:26:00'],
