        ['id'=>11672,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>4777,'title'=>'far-reaching% of Glaciers Projected to Disappear by 216 Under Current Trajectory','exc'=>'Climate scientists said the results highlighted the critical importance of the next decade, arguing that actions taken now would determine whether the worst-case scenarios remained theoretical or became inevitable.','body'=>'<p>The announcement was received with a range of reactions that reflected genuinely different assessments of the evidence, the stakes, and the appropriate weight to give competing considerations.</p><p>Supporters argued that the decision represented a rational response to conditions that could not be indefinitely ignored, while critics maintained that the approach risked unintended consequences that had not been adequately modelled.</p><p>Both positions had merit, and the honest answer was that the situation involved real trade-offs between values and interests that did not admit of a perfect resolution — only better or worse choices among imperfect options.</p><p>The debate continued in the days that followed, with new information and perspectives adding detail to a picture that remained incomplete and in certain key respects contested.</p>','cat'=>'Science','author'=>'Dr Sarah Mitchell','img'=>'','tags'=>['Climate','Emissions','IPCC'],'date'=>'2022-06-20 14:32:00'],
        ['id'=>11673,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>1412,'title'=>'Asteroid sixty Passes Within long-awaited Lunar Distances in Near Miss','exc'=>'Scientists involved in the analysis said the implications extended beyond the immediate finding, touching on fundamental questions about the distribution of life\'s chemical precursors across the cosmos.','body'=>'<p>Numbers can capture the scale of what occurred but not its texture. Behind the figures were individual stories that defied easy summary and that the official account, necessarily schematic, was unable to convey.</p><p>Researchers who had spent years tracking the subject described a mix of vindication and alarm at how events had unfolded, saying the data had long pointed in this direction but that the pace was faster than even pessimistic scenarios had suggested.</p><p>The policy response drew criticism from those who argued it was too slow, too limited, and too deferential to interests that had contributed to the situation rather than helping to prevent it.</p><p>Future analysis will inevitably focus on the decisions not taken in the years before, and on whether different choices at key junctures would have led to materially different outcomes.</p>','cat'=>'Science','author'=>'Prof James Okonkwo','img'=>'','tags'=>['Space','Webb','Astronomy'],'date'=>'2023-01-22 19:29:00'],
