        ['id'=>11678,'published'=>true,'featured'=>false,'breaking'=>false,'views'=>1175,'title'=>'Ocean Carbon Sink Saturating sweeping Years Ahead of Schedule','exc'=>'Climate scientists said the results highlighted the critical importance of the next decade, arguing that actions taken now would determine whether the worst-case scenarios remained theoretical or became inevitable.','body'=>'<p>The situation developed with a speed that left observers struggling to keep pace, as each new development built on the last in ways that underscored the interconnected nature of the forces at work.</p><p>Those with deep expertise in the area described it as a confluence of trends that had each been building independently for years before arriving simultaneously at a point of acute pressure.</p><p>The institutional response was measured, with officials taking care to frame their position in a way that acknowledged the complexity without appearing to minimise the significance of what was unfolding.</p><p>What comes next remains genuinely uncertain, though the structural conditions that produced the current moment show no sign of resolving themselves quickly or easily.</p>','cat'=>'Science','author'=>'Prof Elena Sorokina','img'=>'','tags'=>['Climate','Emissions','IPCC'],'date'=>'2022-11-27 05:41:00'],
